# Go Development Rules

## Package Structure
- Package names: lowercase, short, single word (no underscores)
- cmd/ for entrypoints, internal/ for private packages
- pkg/ for public shared packages
- One package per directory — no mixing

## Types and Structs
- Go has NO classes — use structs + interfaces + composition
- No inheritance — use struct embedding for composition only
- Define interfaces where the consumer lives, not producer
- Small focused interfaces (1-3 methods max)
- All exported types must have godoc comments

## Error Handling
- Always return error as last return value
- Never use _ to ignore errors — always handle
- Wrap errors with context: fmt.Errorf("service.Method: %w", err)
- Custom error types for domain errors (PingError, ComplianceError)
- No panic() in production code — only in main() for startup failures

## Context — Critical for Ping Pipeline
- context.Context must be FIRST parameter of every function doing I/O
- Never store context in struct — always pass as parameter
- Use context.WithTimeout for all external calls
- Ping ingestion: context.WithTimeout(ctx, 100*time.Millisecond) hard limit
- Always check ctx.Done() in long-running goroutine loops

## Goroutines — No Leaks Allowed
- Every goroutine must have a clear exit condition
- Use done channel or context cancellation — never fire-and-forget
- Always handle goroutine panics with recover() in worker pools
- Use sync.WaitGroup to wait for goroutines to finish on shutdown
- Goroutine pool for ping fan-out — never spawn unlimited goroutines

## Graceful Shutdown
- Listen for os.Signal (SIGTERM, SIGINT)
- Stop accepting new pings first
- Wait for in-flight goroutines with WaitGroup (max 30s timeout)
- Flush Kafka producer before exit
- Close DB connections cleanly
- Log "shutdown complete" before exit

## Performance
- sync.Pool for frequently allocated structs in hot ping path
- Redis pipeline for multiple commands in one round trip
- HTTP/2 persistent connections to buyer endpoints
- Avoid interface{} / any in hot path — use concrete types
- Profile with pprof before any optimization

## Health Checks (Required on every service)
- GET /healthz → 200 if process alive (liveness)
- GET /readyz → 200 only if Redis + DB connections healthy (readiness)

## Logging
- Use slog (stdlib) with JSON handler — never fmt.Println
- Every log: trace_id, tenant_id, service, level fields
- Never log caller ANI or any PII